WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.000
Would you rather it in Gaelic or English, or what?

00:03.000 --> 00:05.000
Em, why don’t you tell me a little bit about it

00:05.000 --> 00:07.000
in English first just so I have…

00:07.000 --> 00:15.000
Well this dog he was really clever and the crofter and his wife was,

00:16.000 --> 00:18.000
were planting potatoes.

00:19.000 --> 00:23.000
And they were doing it by spade and they were taking a whole week on the job.

00:24.000 --> 00:31.000
So, the cailleach used to put the tatties 

00:31.000 --> 00:37.000
on the stove ready for when they came home, you see.

00:37.000 --> 00:44.000
And whenever they were properly cooked the dog would come out and start barking. 

00:45.000 --> 00:52.000
But, this day they thought: “well, the tatties should be ready by now,”

00:52.000 --> 00:57.000
but there was no sign of the dog coming out to bark, you know, 

00:57.000 --> 01:04.000
when they were cooked so, they kept looking at the house and, then they saw the dog coming out.

01:05.000 --> 01:11.000
It was a bright sunny day and shielding his eyes with his paws against the sun,

01:11.000 --> 01:16.000
so he could see where they were, to call them back in, you know.

01:16.000 --> 01:19.000
And the cailleach says: “Oh I think we’d better go in and investigate.”

01:20.000 --> 01:30.000
When she went, when she came home the potatoes were burnt to a cinder in the pan.

01:30.000 --> 01:34.000
She wondered, “Oh I wonder what happened to the dog today,”

01:34.000 --> 01:38.000
never realised the tatties were ready. 

01:38.000 --> 01:45.000
So when they looked the clock had stopped. [laughs]

01:46.000 --> 01:52.000
[laughs] Ohh, that’s very good. Do you want to give that one in Gaelic?

01:52.000 --> 01:54.000
That’s quite good.

01:55.000 --> 02:03.000
Aig àm a’ Chogaidh bha, bha airmen ann an campa shìos ann a sheo

02:03.000 --> 02:09.000
agus ’s e Sasannaich a bh’ annta, anns a’ chuid mhòr dhiubh co-dhiù,

02:09.000 --> 02:16.000
cha robh Gàidhlig aca. Agus bha dà chailleach anns a’ bhaile co-dhiù

02:16.000 --> 02:21.000
’s bha iad air cearcan fhaighinn. ’S ann a thòisich na cearcan,

02:21.000 --> 02:24.000
’s ann bhon aon duine a bha iad air na cearcan fhaighinn 

02:24.000 --> 02:26.000
agus thòisich na cearcan a’ dol dhan aon bhothan.

02:26.000 --> 02:32.000
Agus, bha fear aca a bha an-còmhnaidh a’ tighinn a chèilidh air tè dhe na cailleachan 

02:32.000 --> 02:38.000
agus ’s ann a chur iad, ’s ann a chur i an t-airman a dh’iarraidh na cearcan

02:38.000 --> 02:42.000
[XX] gus nach tèideadh iad dhan bhothan-chearc an tè eile.

02:42.000 --> 02:48.000
Ach, co-dhiù, thòisich e gan crodhadh dhachaigh agus tha …

02:50.000 --> 02:54.000
thàinig a’ chailleach eile a-mach a throd ris son a bhith a’ falbh le na cearcan aice.

02:54.000 --> 02:58.000
Agus cha robh esan a’ tuigsinn cò mu dheidhinn an robh i. 

02:58.000 --> 03:01.000
Ach co-dhiù, on ’s e àm a’ Chogaidh a bh’ ann 

03:01.000 --> 03:04.000
bha an t-uabhas de mhinnean a’ tighinn air tìr

03:04.000 --> 03:10.000
air a’ chladach agus bhiodh iad an-còmhnaidh a’ sprèadhadh nuair

03:10.000 --> 03:19.000
a bhualadh iad air a’ chladach, 

03:13.000 --> 03:19.000
agus bhiodh iad a’ cur eagal gu leòr air daoine an uairsin.

03:19.000 --> 03:22.000
An oidhche-sa co-dhiù, mar a bha mi ag ràdh, 

03:22.000 --> 03:25.000
bha a’ chailleach, an t-airman a’ cur a-steach nan cearc 

03:25.000 --> 03:28.000
agus thàinig a’ chailleach a throd ris agus 

03:28.000 --> 03:32.000
cha robh ise ga thuigsinn agus cha robh esan ga tuigsinn

03:32.000 --> 03:36.000
ach rinn e a-mach gur ann a’ feuchainn ri na cearcan a [ruith/?] a bha i,

03:36.000 --> 03:40.000
agus thuirt e rithe: “but they’re mine!”

03:40.000 --> 03:46.000
“Mine,” ars ise, “neo submarine tha mi coma cò aca, leamsa a tha na cearcan.” [laughs]

03:46.000 --> 03:48.000
Did you understand that?

03:48.000 --> 03:52.000
An siud ’s an seo. It happened during the war…

03:51.000 --> 03:52.000
Yes.

03:52.000 --> 03:55.000
And there were, was it a submarine? Or a boat?

03:56.000 --> 04:02.000
No. Airmen were stationed here when the war was on 

04:02.000 --> 04:05.000
and they were English

04:05.000 --> 04:08.000
and they couldn’t understand Gaelic. And these two wifies

04:08.000 --> 04:14.000
they had bought hens from the same source, you know,

04:14.000 --> 04:19.000
and they had a habit of going from one henhouse to the other, 

04:19.000 --> 04:21.000
you know, the whole lot of them.

04:21.000 --> 04:25.000
So this day he was, the airman, this particular airman,

04:25.000 --> 04:27.000
was pally with one of the cailleachs and she told him: 

04:27.000 --> 04:32.000
“go and get the hens before they go into the, the wrong henhouse.”

04:32.000 --> 04:36.000
And he was trying to shoo them back to their own place. 

04:36.000 --> 04:39.000
And the other cailleach came to …

04:39.000 --> 04:43.000
came to warn him against taking her hens, you know,

04:43.000 --> 04:47.000
and he couldn’t understand her and she couldn’t understand him 

04:47.000 --> 04:50.000
and she was crying and shouting at him and, but he says:

04:50.000 --> 04:53.000
“But they’re mine!” and you know, mines were being 

04:53.000 --> 04:55.000
washed ashore here and bursting at all hours

04:55.000 --> 04:57.000
of the morning when the war was on. 

04:57.000 --> 04:59.000
That’s the only thing she understood, you know,

04:59.000 --> 05:05.000
‘mine’, and she says, “mine or submarine, they’re mine!” [laugh]

05:05.000 --> 05:08.000
Ha! That’s very good. Hilarious.

05:08.000 --> 05:11.000
And were those two cailleachs just in here? Near here?

05:11.000 --> 05:13.000
Aye, yes.

05:15.000 --> 05:25.000
Other thing, something about a relation of ours over in Lewis,

05:25.000 --> 05:29.000
and he called there one day and 

05:29.000 --> 05:32.000
the bodach in the house was telling him he found this

05:32.000 --> 05:41.000
plank of wood washed up on the beach and he took it home.

05:42.000 --> 05:48.000
But every night he was hearing some hammering 

05:48.000 --> 05:52.000
or queer noises round about this plank, you know and,

05:52.000 --> 05:59.000
eventually, oh, this was going on for a while and he decided there

05:59.000 --> 06:04.000
was something queer attached to the plank. 

06:04.000 --> 06:09.000
So, he had a cow that was going to calve,

06:10.000 --> 06:20.000
and he went and sawed the plank and 

06:20.000 --> 06:22.000
put it underneath the cow, you know,

06:22.000 --> 06:27.000
to put bedding on top. So he says ‘thats the end of the plank anyway’.

06:27.000 --> 06:34.000
But shortly after that, somebody died suddenly,

06:34.000 --> 06:37.000
and at that time they were making the coffins themselves

06:37.000 --> 06:41.000
and they couldn’t find a suitable piece of wood,

06:41.000 --> 06:45.000
you know, to make the coffin, so they had to rip the

06:45.000 --> 06:52.000
cow’s stall out to get the wood for the coffin.

06:54.000 --> 07:00.000
So, it’s probably quite true. Things like that do happen.

07:04.000 --> 07:07.000
…cluinntinn aig m’ athair, bodach a bhuineadh dha thall ann an Leòdhas

07:07.000 --> 07:17.000
agus bha e an triop-sa a’ dol a dh’iarraidh bò agus ’s ann a… 

07:17.000 --> 07:18.000
thadhal e son an oidhche a thoirt seachad 

07:18.000 --> 07:20.000
còmhla ris a’ bhodach agus bha e ag innse dha

07:20.000 --> 07:24.000
gun d’ fhuair e plank air a’ chladach, agus thug e dhachaigh e.

07:25.000 --> 07:28.000
Ach goirid às dèidh sin ’s ann a thòisich e air cluinntinn fuaimean annasach

07:28.000 --> 07:32.000
timcheall air a’ phlank a h-uile oidhche agus, 

07:33.000 --> 07:37.000
och cha do shaoil e càil dhe an toiseach ach bha e ga chluinntinn gu math tric

07:37.000 --> 07:41.000
is smaoinich e mu dheireadh gun robh rudeigin timcheall air a’ phlank

07:41.000 --> 07:45.000
a bha seo agus ’s ann a dh’fhalbh e agus shàbh e e agus 

07:45.000 --> 07:47.000
rinn e stàile dhan a’ bhoin leis.

07:48.000 --> 07:53.000
Agus thuirt e ris fhin, ge b’ e [XX] bh’ ann, cha chuir e an còrr dragh.

07:53.000 --> 07:55.000
Ach goirid às dèidh sin ’s ann a bhàsaich cuideigin,

07:55.000 --> 07:59.000
aithghearr, a bha anns a’ bhaile agus bhiodh iad fhèin

07:59.000 --> 08:02.000
a’ dèanamh nan cistichean-laighe an uairsin agus cha robh…

08:02.000 --> 08:04.000
cha robh fiodh ri fhaighinn 

08:04.000 --> 08:09.000
agus thàinig orra am plank a a thoirt às a… stàile na bà 

08:09.000 --> 08:12.000
agus a’ chiste-laighe a dhèanamh dhi.

08:16.000 --> 08:24.000
This cailleach in Uibhist, agus bha i… 

08:24.000 --> 08:28.000
cha bhiodh i rèidh ri duine dhe na nàbaidhean.

08:28.000 --> 08:34.000
Bhiodh i an-còmhnaidh a’ trod ri cuideigin. Ach, latha-sa co-dhiù,

08:35.000 --> 08:39.000
’s ann a dh’fhàs a’ chailleach bhochd tinn agus bhàsaich i.

08:39.000 --> 08:42.000
Agus ’s ann anns a’ Gheamhradh a bh’ ann

08:42.000 --> 08:44.000
nuair a bha iad ga tiodhlacadh, bha iad a’ falbh 

08:44.000 --> 08:48.000
dhan chladh leis a’ chailleach a thiodhlacadh agus ’s ann a …

08:48.000 --> 08:53.000
bha cnaicearachd de dealanaich is tàirneanaich ann.

08:53.000 --> 08:56.000
Thuirt an dàrna fear ris an fhear eile dhen fheadhainn

08:56.000 --> 09:01.000
a bha aig a’ chladh: “Feumaidh gun d’ ràinig i.” [laughs]

09:01.000 --> 09:03.000
Now go on, tell me.

09:03.000 --> 09:06.000
Now this old cailleach she was forever rowing with the neighbours.

09:08.000 --> 09:15.000
And she was forever causing a disturbance in the village and, 

09:15.000 --> 09:19.000
och she died eventually and they went to bury her and it was

09:19.000 --> 09:27.000
winter time, and when they were leaving the cemetery there was an

09:27.000 --> 09:30.000
awful peal of thunder and lightning.

09:31.000 --> 09:34.000
And one of the bodachs said to the other: 

09:34.000 --> 09:39.000
“Oh, she must have reached.” [laughs]

09:43.000 --> 09:47.000
I know when I was on Barra that I would hear, 

09:47.000 --> 09:51.000
or get to hear a few stories about Mingulay, 

09:51.000 --> 09:54.000
and some of the other islands out around Barra.

09:55.000 --> 09:59.000
And I wondered, do you, because in Uist,

09:59.000 --> 10:02.000
I hear stories like Rona, right enough, 

10:01.000 --> 10:02.000
I heard some stories about Rona

10:02.000 --> 10:05.000
and I know that there are some folk on Fladda 

10:05.000 --> 10:07.000
that have some stories, em, about Fladda.

10:08.000 --> 10:12.000
But it’s been hard to try and find stories about, say,

10:12.000 --> 10:16.000
the Monachs, or Heisker and St Kilda.

10:14.000 --> 10:16.000
Aye aye.

10:16.000 --> 10:19.000
Now, because you, and when I’m on Berneray I hear Splash knows

10:19.000 --> 10:21.000
a few stories about Pabbay, 

10:21.000 --> 10:24.000
but there’s quite a few islands around Harris.

10:23.000 --> 10:24.000
Yes.

10:24.000 --> 10:26.000
And they used to, quite a few of them were habit- were…

10:26.000 --> 10:28.000
Oh yes, nearly every one of them.

10:28.000 --> 10:30.000
Do you have stories from any of the islands?

10:30.000 --> 10:31.000
Pardon?

10:31.000 --> 10:35.000
Did you ever hear any, are there any stories left from those islands 

10:35.000 --> 10:40.000
about something that happened to folk there or about… because, people lived

10:40.000 --> 10:43.000
there quite a long time ago so there might be stories of battles

10:43.000 --> 10:48.000
or whatever that has happened on some of them … 

10:46.000 --> 10:48.000
or St Kilda for that matter.

10:48.000 --> 10:53.000
Oh yes. There was one about Taransay.

10:55.000 --> 11:01.000
The Morrisons from Ness, they used to come raiding down here, you know.

11:01.000 --> 11:08.000
This time they came to Taransay and they were

11:08.000 --> 11:12.000
raiding cattle and horses and all that.

11:13.000 --> 11:17.000
And at that time they were fighting with bow and arrows

11:17.000 --> 11:20.000
and they chased this fellow and he stood on a rock,

11:20.000 --> 11:23.000
just opposite Seilebost there.

11:23.000 --> 11:28.000
They chased him on to the shore and he jumped on to this rock

11:28.000 --> 11:39.000
and one of the, one of the Morrisons pranged him with an arrow

11:39.000 --> 11:42.000
and it stuck right in his bottom!

11:42.000 --> 11:47.000
He jumped into the sea and he swam to Seilebost and they didn’t,

11:47.000 --> 11:49.000
they didn’t get him anyway.

11:50.000 --> 11:54.000
But, oh about twenty years afterwards he went,

11:54.000 --> 11:57.000
he went up to Ness for a horse

11:58.000 --> 12:05.000
and they were all wearing, probably, skin hide kilts at that time,

12:05.000 --> 12:09.000
and so he went to this house and he was going to stay for the night.

12:10.000 --> 12:17.000
And, oh, he was suspicious of this bodach in the house,

12:18.000 --> 12:21.000
he thought he knew him, you know.

12:21.000 --> 12:23.000
It was him that had shot him!

12:25.000 --> 12:30.000
Oh he studied this thing for a while and thought to himself, you know,

12:30.000 --> 12:35.000
I won’t part with him without telling him who I am, you see.

12:35.000 --> 12:38.000
So before he went to bed, he says:

12:38.000 --> 12:42.000
‘we’ve got a habit in Taransay of warming your bottom 

12:42.000 --> 12:44.000
against the fire, you know, before we go to bed.’

12:44.000 --> 12:47.000
So he lifted up his kilt, you know, and the other fellow 

12:47.000 --> 12:51.000
saw the scar [laughs] and he says, 

12:51.000 --> 12:57.000
‘oh, I think we should forget about that now.’ [laughs]

12:59.000 --> 13:02.000
Very good. Aye, go on and tell it in Gaelic.

13:04.000 --> 13:09.000
Bha uair a bha na Moireasdanaich ann an Nis bha iad a’ tighinn a-nuas

13:09.000 --> 13:13.000
ann a sheo a ghoid chruidh is chaorach is each 

13:13.000 --> 13:15.000
agus rud sam bith a gheibheadh iad.

13:15.000 --> 13:18.000
Ach an triop seo chaidh iad air tìr ann an Tarasaigh agus 

13:18.000 --> 13:26.000
’s e bogha is saighead a bhiodh aca an uairsin ri cogadh

13:26.000 --> 13:31.000
is bha iad a’ ruith nan Tarasach co-dhiù agus

13:31.000 --> 13:40.000
leum fear aca gu clach, dìreach [XX] Tharasaigh 

13:38.000 --> 13:40.000
air tìr air a bheil ‘Sgeir Leum’ aca.

13:41.000 --> 13:49.000
Agus loisg fear air le bogha is saighead 

13:45.000 --> 13:49.000
agus chuir e an treagh na thòin.

13:49.000 --> 13:53.000
Agus leum e a-mach air a’ mhuir is shnàmh e, 

13:53.000 --> 13:55.000
shnàmh e an caolas is chaidh e air tìr

13:55.000 --> 14:01.000
ann an Horgabost air sgeir air a bheil ‘Sgeir Eòghainn’ aca 

14:01.000 --> 14:02.000
chun an latha an-diugh.

14:03.000 --> 14:10.000
Agus, thug e an treagh às a thòin an uairsin agus chàir e e agus…

14:11.000 --> 14:13.000
ach bliadhnaichean mòra às dèidh sin ’s ann a 

14:13.000 --> 14:17.000
chaidh e suas a Nis a lorg each

14:18.000 --> 14:23.000
agus an taigh dhan deach e son fàthachd na h-oidhche,

14:23.000 --> 14:25.000
’s ann a rinn e a-mach gun robh e ag aithneachadh 

14:25.000 --> 14:27.000
a’ bhodaich a bha san taigh,

14:27.000 --> 14:31.000
agus gur h-e a bha air losgadh air ann an Tarasaigh.

14:32.000 --> 14:35.000
Cha robh e cinnteach gu dè dhèanadh e co-dhiù ach

14:35.000 --> 14:39.000
bha e tàmailteach leis falbh gun leigeadh fhaicinn dha cò bh’ ann agus 

14:39.000 --> 14:44.000
’s e èileadh a bh’ air, èileadh craicinn, tha mi creids. 

14:45.000 --> 14:48.000
Agus mun an deach iad a chadal, gun tuirt e ris: 

14:49.000 --> 14:54.000
‘tha fasan againn ann an Tarasaigh a bhith dèanamh rud air a bheil 

14:54.000 --> 14:57.000
garadh cùl-chas aca mun tid sinn dhan leabaidh’

14:57.000 --> 15:03.000
agus thog e an t-èileadh air a thòin agus nuair a mhothaich am bodach Niseach

15:03.000 --> 15:07.000
dhan làrach an lot a bh’ air, thuirt e ris:

15:07.000 --> 15:10.000
“Oh ’s fheàrr dhut a dhol a sheo dha do leabaidh, 

15:10.000 --> 15:13.000
chan ann droch choinneachadh seo idir.”

15:15.000 --> 15:17.000
Go from the start, that’s your family?

15:18.000 --> 15:21.000
Aye, Clann ’illInnein ’s ann à ’ic an t-Sàil a thàinig iad,

15:21.000 --> 15:28.000
thàinig iad nan cìobairean a Phabaigh agus 

15:28.000 --> 15:29.000
bha iad ann am Pabaigh,

15:29.000 --> 15:32.000
bha tòrr dhaoine ann am Pabaigh an uairsin agus bhiodh iad,

15:32.000 --> 15:37.000
bhiodh iad a’ dèanamh uisge-beatha ann.

15:38.000 --> 15:41.000
Agus bhiodh na gèidsearan, ’s e a bh’ aca orra an uairsin,

15:42.000 --> 15:45.000
bha amharas aca gun robh iad a’ [XX]

15:45.000 --> 15:48.000
’s gun robh iad a’ tighinn a rannsachadh an eilein 

15:48.000 --> 15:51.000
agus am fear a bha a’ dèanamh an aiseig, 

15:51.000 --> 15:53.000
am fear a bha a’ dèanamh an aiseig bhiodh e,

15:54.000 --> 15:58.000
bha dà sheòl aige san eathar: fear ruadh agus fear geal.

15:58.000 --> 16:01.000
Agus nuair a bhiodh duin’ ann nach robh e ag aithneachadh 

16:01.000 --> 16:05.000
bhiodh e a’ cur an dàrna chuid, am fear geal air neo am fear dubh,

16:05.000 --> 16:08.000
eh an fhear ruadh, an àird 

16:08.000 --> 16:11.000
gus an aithneachadh iad ann am Pabaigh gun robh 

16:11.000 --> 16:14.000
cuideigin de shrainnsear air bòrd aige.

16:15.000 --> 16:19.000
Ach bhiodh iad an uairsin air a h-uile càil fhalach mun [freakadh/?] daoine.

16:20.000 --> 16:24.000
Agus, an triop-sa ’s ann bhrist fear an aiseig,

16:24.000 --> 16:28.000
bhrist e a chas anns a’ chladach agus 

16:28.000 --> 16:32.000
’s e cuideigineach eile a bha a’ dèanamh an aiseig 

16:32.000 --> 16:38.000
agus thàinig an gèidsear ann gun fhiosd agus an seòl ceàrr aige

16:38.000 --> 16:42.000
an àird agus shàr e iad leis an uisge-beatha 

16:42.000 --> 16:45.000
agus chaidh an clearaigeadh a-mach air Pabaigh an uairsin.

16:46.000 --> 16:49.000
Chaidh feadhainn aca a Tharasaigh, is feadhainn a Scalpaigh agus…

16:51.000 --> 16:57.000
sin na daoine dhan robh Clann ’illInnein

16:57.000 --> 17:00.000
bho bheil mise agus a h-uile duine eile an tùs.

17:00.000 --> 17:04.000
Thàinig iad às a seo nuair chaidh an sgapadh agus 

17:04.000 --> 17:09.000
sguir iad ga dhèanamh, ach cha do sguir iad ga òl idir!

17:12.000 --> 17:15.000
About the fairies at Luskentyre?

17:15.000 --> 17:19.000
And Luran, he was the shepherd?

17:20.000 --> 17:24.000
He was watching, he had the sheep in the hillock.

17:24.000 --> 17:26.000
On the side of the hill there’s

17:26.000 --> 17:28.000
kind of a shelter, a wee knoll,

17:28.000 --> 17:33.000
well a hollow actually, and the fairies came out

17:33.000 --> 17:35.000
and were pinching his sheep.

17:35.000 --> 17:37.000
No, I’ve never heard that.

17:35.000 --> 17:38.000
You’ve never heard that? Oh, its quite good,

17:38.000 --> 17:42.000
I don’t know it well enough to… but,

17:42.000 --> 17:46.000
it’s about this fairy and Luran was the shepherd,

17:47.000 --> 17:50.000
then somehow or another Luran ends up

17:50.000 --> 17:52.000
stealing their wee cup that they drink out of,

17:52.000 --> 17:54.000
they were having a wee party.

17:53.000 --> 17:54.000
Yes.

17:54.000 --> 17:57.000
And he steals the cup and they can’t catch him

17:57.000 --> 18:00.000
because he’s very fast, and that’s why the wee rhyme is:

18:00.000 --> 18:04.000
Luran the fast-footed one. And…

18:04.000 --> 18:07.000
Oh I’ve heard the rhyme right enough but…

18:07.000 --> 18:09.000
Yes, and so the fairies start singing,

18:09.000 --> 18:14.000
start saying a rhyme about how Luran eats the hard brea-

18:14.000 --> 18:17.000
bread, so he’s very fast, but if he eats the

18:17.000 --> 18:21.000
soft porridge, he’ll be even faster.

18:21.000 --> 18:23.000
So it tricks him and he thinks, right, 

18:23.000 --> 18:26.000
if he starts eating soft porridge, he’ll be faster yet.

18:26.000 --> 18:28.000
So he eats soft porridge and he gets fat and 

18:28.000 --> 18:31.000
he gets really lazy and slow. [laughs]

18:31.000 --> 18:33.000
And then the fairies are able to catch him!

18:33.000 --> 18:34.000
[laughs] Aye aye!

18:34.000 --> 18:38.000
It’s quite a good, quite a clever wee story about the fairies,

18:38.000 --> 18:42.000
and I don’t hear a lot of fairy stories, every now and again.

18:43.000 --> 18:45.000
…solicitor in Edinburgh. 

18:46.000 --> 18:53.000
And he was, in lee with the Jacobites at the Jacobite Rising

18:53.000 --> 19:01.000
and she was anti, very much anti-Jacobite.

19:02.000 --> 19:08.000
And this time they were having a meeting in the house,

19:08.000 --> 19:12.000
in the solicitor’s house, and of course 

19:12.000 --> 19:17.000
she was excluded from their presence there. 

19:18.000 --> 19:20.000
And she hid behind a sofa,

19:23.000 --> 19:32.000
but eventually they noticed she was there and they…

19:33.000 --> 19:45.000
her husband got, got… somebody to take her away 

19:45.000 --> 19:48.000
and imprison her in…

19:50.000 --> 19:55.000
oh I can’t remember what island was she on at first?

19:56.000 --> 20:02.000
And, he went around his friends then a couple of days

20:02.000 --> 20:06.000
after that and told them she had died,

20:06.000 --> 20:10.000
and they held a mock funeral in Edinburgh,

20:10.000 --> 20:21.000
and… He then, she had been put, I can’t remember which one

20:21.000 --> 20:23.000
of the islands she was imprisoned on.

20:23.000 --> 20:29.000
And then, she was years there and she got transferred 

20:29.000 --> 20:32.000
to St Kilda in case they caught up with her.

20:33.000 --> 20:41.000
And she spent her whole time there in a wee bothy on her own,

20:41.000 --> 20:44.000
and the St Kildans were feeding her.

20:44.000 --> 20:48.000
You know, they were given her their share of the birds and everything

20:48.000 --> 20:50.000
they were getting themselves.

20:50.000 --> 20:53.000
But she, she didn’t mix with them at all,

20:53.000 --> 20:56.000
she was very much a loner there and 

20:56.000 --> 20:59.000
she wasn’t friendly by any means.

20:59.000 --> 21:05.000
But she used to send the St Kildan tweeds to market

21:05.000 --> 21:08.000
down in London or somewhere down south there,

21:09.000 --> 21:17.000
and she used to put a slip of paper in the roll of tweed, you know,

21:18.000 --> 21:24.000
to let somebody know that she was, she was there for years

21:24.000 --> 21:28.000
but nobody took any notice of her.

21:28.000 --> 21:36.000
And then, the authorities got to know she was there and 

21:36.000 --> 21:40.000
they staged a second burial for her on Skye,

21:42.000 --> 21:51.000
and eventually she was taken back to St Kilda,

21:51.000 --> 21:54.000
I don’t know where she actually eventually died,

21:55.000 --> 21:59.000
but she had a terrible time of it.

22:03.000 --> 22:07.000
Lady Grange, she was called. 

22:09.000 --> 22:12.000
So she was buried three times.

22:13.000 --> 22:16.000
That’s a sad story, aye.

22:17.000 --> 22:21.000
Well, the outer wall was taken from Aberdeen, and the

22:21.000 --> 22:30.000
inner wall, and the stone in the breakwater came from Bernera.

22:32.000 --> 22:35.000
It was built in two stages, 

22:36.000 --> 22:41.000
it was built by the Earl of Dunmore.

22:42.000 --> 22:48.000
And he was engaged to this society lady,

22:48.000 --> 22:50.000
I don’t know who she was,

22:50.000 --> 22:54.000
but when the castle was finished,

22:55.000 --> 22:59.000
it was half the size of what it is now, the original castle.

22:59.000 --> 23:03.000
You’ll notice that when you’re passing, you’ll see it’s in…

23:04.000 --> 23:10.000
And when it was finished she came to have a look at it and she said 

23:10.000 --> 23:14.000
its more like the henhouse my father has,

23:14.000 --> 23:17.000
so she wouldn’t take anything more to do with it,

23:17.000 --> 23:20.000
so he added as much again to it.

23:22.000 --> 23:28.000
And by this time he was bankrupt and…

23:29.000 --> 23:32.000
I don’t think they got married at all.

23:32.000 --> 23:44.000
Anyway, he was filed for bankruptcy and the Scotts 

23:44.000 --> 23:49.000
who had it after the Lord Dunmore,

23:49.000 --> 23:53.000
they were merchant bankers in Edinburgh

23:53.000 --> 23:58.000
who had given them an overdraft for the building.

23:59.000 --> 24:06.000
And in lieu of the money they got the castle.

24:06.000 --> 24:11.000
And that was Sir Edward Scott. 

24:12.000 --> 24:22.000
And then when Edward Scott died his son inherited the estate.

24:23.000 --> 24:32.000
Think its 165,000 acres and he had it for a few years

24:33.000 --> 24:36.000
and he decided to sell it,

24:37.000 --> 24:42.000
and shortly after that, a few years after that, ach he wasn’t…

24:44.000 --> 24:47.000
he changed his mind again, and the next time it came on the market

24:47.000 --> 24:54.000
he bought it again and he died in…

24:55.000 --> 25:00.000
oh, during the war, ’42 or ’43. 

25:00.000 --> 25:03.000
Then it was bought by Sir Thomas Sopwith,

25:04.000 --> 25:08.000
the famous aircraft manufacturer.

25:09.000 --> 25:13.000
But it’s changed hands a good few times since.

25:16.000 --> 25:23.000
So, Samuel Scott is buried in a cairn,

25:23.000 --> 25:25.000
on the hilltop above the castle,

25:25.000 --> 25:27.000
you’ll probably notice it on your way down.

25:28.000 --> 25:32.000
There’s a big stone cairn there.

25:33.000 --> 25:39.000
And four years ago, lightning struck it

25:39.000 --> 25:47.000
and punched a big hole in the roof and it’s built like a [cone/?],

25:48.000 --> 25:52.000
of solid granite and a lead roof on it,

25:52.000 --> 25:56.000
and the inside is lined with lead.

25:56.000 --> 25:59.000
And the lightning struck a,

25:59.000 --> 26:01.000
punched a big hole through it and

26:01.000 --> 26:09.000
the caskets with the Lady Scott and Samuel Scott’s ashes were

26:11.000 --> 26:18.000
in the wee alcove in the walls, but the lightning,

26:19.000 --> 26:23.000
the caskets being metal, you know, it was attracted by lightning

26:23.000 --> 26:25.000
and burst open the caskets,

26:25.000 --> 26:28.000
and you can still see the ashes lying there in a heap.

26:30.000 --> 26:32.000
Its been there ever since.

26:33.000 --> 26:37.000
[...]

26:37.000 --> 26:44.000
So the, the estate proprietor just now,

26:44.000 --> 26:50.000
he was going to repair it but he was looking for a grant 

26:50.000 --> 26:52.000
for it and he didn’t get the grant so 

26:52.000 --> 26:57.000
its still lying in bits and pieces there.

26:58.000 --> 27:00.000
Who owns it now?

27:01.000 --> 27:04.000
Oh, I don’t know if anybody owns it.

27:04.000 --> 27:06.000
Is it lived in?

27:06.000 --> 27:09.000
The castle, you mean? Oh aye aye,

27:09.000 --> 27:18.000
oh its owned by the cider people. Bulmer, aye.

27:18.000 --> 27:23.000
Huh. I didn’t know that about the mausoleum,

27:23.000 --> 27:26.000
the wee, the cairn, that’s quite something.

27:26.000 --> 27:28.000
Do you get many lightning storms here?

27:28.000 --> 27:31.000
No, not really, no.

27:31.000 --> 27:33.000
Ah, that’s quite something. Quite something.

27:34.000 --> 27:36.000
But it’s a pity they left it like that.

27:36.000 --> 27:39.000
They should… somebody should have…

27:39.000 --> 27:42.000
They could have done something anyways, gee whiz.

27:42.000 --> 27:46.000
Och it wouldn’t cost that much to repair it.

27:44.000 --> 27:46.000
No.

27:46.000 --> 27:52.000
But I suppose, many’s a hard time he had at sea,

27:52.000 --> 27:54.000
he was in the Navy during the war.

27:55.000 --> 27:58.000
The First World War, of course.

28:01.000 --> 28:06.000
But, I never heard… 

28:06.000 --> 28:10.000
I rememember just before the war, before the last war,

28:10.000 --> 28:23.000
1935 I think, a big sailing ship on its way from Norway

28:26.000 --> 28:30.000
to some English port got wrecked on St Kilda.

28:30.000 --> 28:35.000
It was a three-masted scooner. 

28:36.000 --> 28:43.000
She hit St Kilda in thick, in thick fog.

28:44.000 --> 28:51.000
But they managed to stem the flow of water and then sailed on,

28:51.000 --> 28:53.000
and then it ran aground in [XX]. 

28:54.000 --> 28:58.000
And… I think it was seven,

28:58.000 --> 29:07.000
skeleton crew that was, it was some English titled lady that bought it

29:07.000 --> 29:14.000
and it was a skeleton crew that was sailing it.

29:14.000 --> 29:18.000
But it got wrecked on [XX] and they came ashore,

29:18.000 --> 29:24.000
they came ashore, not on [XX], I think it was round Northton

29:24.000 --> 29:26.000
or somewhere round there, they came ashore,

29:26.000 --> 29:32.000
they came ashore with their canvas dinghy.

29:34.000 --> 29:43.000
And then the gales broke it up and the coast here

29:43.000 --> 29:45.000
was littered with wreckage.

29:47.000 --> 29:58.000
And I remember the, some of the hoops that were round the mast,

29:58.000 --> 30:02.000
wooden hoops for running the sail up.

30:02.000 --> 30:06.000
And they were seven foot diameter,

30:06.000 --> 30:09.000
so it must have been an awful size.

30:11.000 --> 30:13.000
And there are still bits and pieces of it lying 

30:13.000 --> 30:15.000
around the shore here.

30:18.000 --> 30:23.000
Whatever happened to them after that, I’m not sure.

30:26.000 --> 30:28.000
…Fit them and all the rest of it.

30:29.000 --> 30:34.000
They went to this cailleachan and her neighbour was in and,

30:34.000 --> 30:39.000
oh he showed her the gas mask and he wanted to give her

30:39.000 --> 30:41.000
a demonstration of how to wear it, 

30:41.000 --> 30:47.000
and they fitted her up with it and the other cailleach shouted:

30:47.000 --> 30:51.000
‘Oh, leave it on, she looks better with it!’ [laughs]

30:55.000 --> 30:58.000
What was it like here during the war?

30:59.000 --> 31:06.000
Well, for us it was pretty interesting.

31:08.000 --> 31:13.000
We were, when the war started we were teenagers then

31:13.000 --> 31:21.000
and all sorts of junk used to be washed up on the shore.

31:22.000 --> 31:29.000
We used to get everything from mines to biscuits and jam 

31:29.000 --> 31:31.000
washed up on the shore every morning.

31:32.000 --> 31:35.000
So that was the first thing we did,

31:35.000 --> 31:40.000
come daylight, head for the shore and see what we would find.

31:41.000 --> 31:46.000
And, it’s a wonder we didn’t get killed long ago.

31:47.000 --> 31:50.000
One time we found an incendiary bomb

31:50.000 --> 31:52.000
and we threw it over a cliff and it

31:52.000 --> 31:57.000
burned there for a couple of days.

31:58.000 --> 32:00.000
We were just curious as to what it…

32:00.000 --> 32:04.000
we guessed it was some kind of bomb, right enough, but,

32:04.000 --> 32:07.000
we were lucky it was an incendiary bomb.

32:11.000 --> 32:18.000
And there used to be tonnes and tonnes of timber washed up,

32:19.000 --> 32:24.000
Oregon pine and all this kind of wood.

32:25.000 --> 32:28.000
It was a, there was an awful lot of 

32:28.000 --> 32:33.000
peat props and logs of every description.

32:34.000 --> 32:37.000
We used to saw it up and burn it,

32:37.000 --> 32:40.000
you couldn’t do anything else with it, really.

32:40.000 --> 32:43.000
Everybody had more than enough of it.

32:46.000 --> 32:54.000
And then you used to get cases of field rations, you know,

32:54.000 --> 32:57.000
tins like, something like cocoa tins,

32:57.000 --> 33:04.000
but they were sealed and there was five biscuits,

33:04.000 --> 33:10.000
rich tea biscuits and, or digestives biscuits and

33:10.000 --> 33:16.000
a spot of coffee and a slab of chocolate.

33:17.000 --> 33:27.000
And two… three boiling sweets,

33:28.000 --> 33:34.000
and there was something else in it too…

33:34.000 --> 33:38.000
Ovaltine, so they were in great demand

33:38.000 --> 33:42.000
if you got them at that time. Yes. 

33:44.000 --> 33:53.000
And cases of lard, used to get 56 pound cases of pure lard,

33:54.000 --> 33:57.000
and it was quite good too!

33:59.000 --> 34:02.000
Everybody was… in fact, we were feeding it

34:02.000 --> 34:08.000
to the cows, and what a polish their…

34:08.000 --> 34:13.000
their coat had after eating lard all winter.

34:16.000 --> 34:20.000
Och, you could never guess what was washed up.

34:21.000 --> 34:28.000
You know, lifeboats and bits of aeroplanes and…

34:28.000 --> 34:30.000
How did you… How did you share it out

34:30.000 --> 34:33.000
or if you found it then, if you found a whole case of something,

34:33.000 --> 34:35.000
was it yours or did you share it out?

34:35.000 --> 34:37.000
Och everybody got enough of it,

34:37.000 --> 34:41.000
there was enough there for everybody, yes.

34:43.000 --> 34:52.000
Aviation fuel, we didn’t buy a spot of paraffin

34:52.000 --> 34:58.000
right through the war, we got an awful lot of aviation fuel.

34:58.000 --> 35:04.000
And there was ‘use no air pressure’ stamped 

35:04.000 --> 35:06.000
on top of the 40 gallon drums,

35:06.000 --> 35:09.000
and the first thing we were doing is putting it

35:09.000 --> 35:12.000
into the tilly lamp and pumping it!

35:13.000 --> 35:16.000
But, nobody ever came to grief over it.

35:19.000 --> 35:23.000
There was very little spirits like whisky or anything like that,

35:23.000 --> 35:26.000
if you got a lifeboat and it was [whole/?] there

35:26.000 --> 35:28.000
was a bottle of rum in it but…

35:28.000 --> 35:36.000
apart from that there was no spirits being washed up at all.

35:37.000 --> 35:39.000
Not like the Politican.

35:39.000 --> 35:41.000
Apart from the Politican in Uist.

35:43.000 --> 35:45.000
I’ve got a Politican bottle.

35:46.000 --> 35:47.000
Pardon?

35:47.000 --> 35:51.000
I’ve got a Politican bottle! Its empty.

35:51.000 --> 36:04.000
[laughs] Oh aye, I had a dram of that Politican whisky, right enough. Yes.

36:07.000 --> 36:11.000
And you said before that it…

36:11.000 --> 36:15.000
in the morning the news would come on and there was one person 

36:15.000 --> 36:17.000
that had a wireless and…

36:17.000 --> 36:20.000
Oh aye everybody flocked into the…

36:20.000 --> 36:22.000
any house that had a wireless.

36:22.000 --> 36:25.000
How close did the fighting get to here?

36:26.000 --> 36:29.000
Oh well we never saw any of it,

36:29.000 --> 36:35.000
but as far as I know the only…

36:37.000 --> 36:41.000
time anything happened here was when a German aircraft

36:41.000 --> 36:44.000
machine-gunned the lighthouse at Ness.

36:46.000 --> 36:49.000
And it tried to extinguish the light.

36:51.000 --> 36:54.000
And, not so long ago since they took the lenses

36:54.000 --> 37:02.000
off the lighthouse and a chap who bought

37:02.000 --> 37:05.000
the lighthouse in Scalpay got them.

37:08.000 --> 37:11.000
The… they were marked, the lenses were marked

37:11.000 --> 37:13.000
with the machine gun bullets.

37:13.000 --> 37:20.000
When they changed the lighthouse to automatic at Ness,

37:20.000 --> 37:23.000
they took these lenses out and put another kind in,

37:23.000 --> 37:25.000
and that chap in Scalpay got them.

37:26.000 --> 37:29.000
Got them for a song.

37:31.000 --> 37:33.000
Did any German… German prisoners,

37:33.000 --> 37:35.000
Germans ever land on the island?

37:35.000 --> 37:37.000
No, oh no. 

37:41.000 --> 37:43.000
No, but they were lurking round here,

37:43.000 --> 37:48.000
the submarines, right enough, because they…

37:50.000 --> 37:53.000
armed merchant crews are called the Asturias,

37:54.000 --> 37:56.000
sunk one just off Dunvegan.

37:58.000 --> 38:03.000
There was a fisherman out fishing out of Stocainis

38:04.000 --> 38:09.000
and the periscope came up within shouting distance to them, 

38:09.000 --> 38:12.000
and they reported it.

38:12.000 --> 38:19.000
And the, this cruiser Asturias was in the area somewhere

38:19.000 --> 38:26.000
and she depth-charged it off Dunvegan somewhere or other.

38:29.000 --> 38:31.000
So that’s as near as the

38:31.000 --> 38:36.000
Germans got to here, right enough.

38:39.000 --> 38:42.000
But you could never tell where they were.

38:45.000 --> 38:49.000
That time I was in St Kilda,

38:50.000 --> 38:54.000
a cousin of mine phoned from Inverness.

38:54.000 --> 39:00.000
She was a school teacher and she phoned from Inverness here

39:00.000 --> 39:03.000
and Seonag says to her, “Calum’s away in St Kilda.”

39:03.000 --> 39:07.000
“Oh,” she says, “what a pity I wasn’t in Harris,”

39:08.000 --> 39:16.000
and she says, “In fact, I’ve got a German teacher whose

39:16.000 --> 39:22.000
on an exchange, a scheme, in just now, and we 

39:22.000 --> 39:26.000
were just talking about St Kilda.”

39:26.000 --> 39:28.000
So I says… Seonag says to her,

39:28.000 --> 39:31.000
“well that’s interesting,” she says.

39:33.000 --> 39:39.000
She said: “her father was the commander of a submarine

39:39.000 --> 39:44.000
that shelled-” Ah, no! Sorry!

39:44.000 --> 39:49.000
“That… ran aground in the bay in St Kilda.”

39:50.000 --> 39:56.000
And the, previously, 

39:56.000 --> 40:01.000
a submarine had shelled the wireless station in St Kilda.

40:01.000 --> 40:04.000
He shouted to the St Kildans to clear off first,

40:04.000 --> 40:09.000
and then he fired 72 shells into the wireless station.

40:10.000 --> 40:16.000
But, at that time, shorly after that when the

40:16.000 --> 40:18.000
authorities got to know about it, they…

40:19.000 --> 40:32.000
they sent some, probably a destroyer or a few of them 

40:32.000 --> 40:35.000
out to St Kilda to look for this submarine.

40:35.000 --> 40:40.000
And they tracked this one down and started 

40:40.000 --> 40:43.000
depth-charging it and it wasn’t the one that shelled the 

40:43.000 --> 40:46.000
station, but another one.

40:47.000 --> 40:49.000
And it sprang a leak,

40:49.000 --> 40:53.000
but they never finished it off,

40:53.000 --> 40:59.000
and it was leaking that bad when it got dark 

40:59.000 --> 41:01.000
they sneaked into St Kilda bay

41:01.000 --> 41:08.000
and beached it to try and repair it.

41:10.000 --> 41:13.000
And when they, when daylight came there was a

41:13.000 --> 41:17.000
big monstrous cannon right on the hill facing them!

41:17.000 --> 41:20.000
So they didn't know what on earth to do

41:20.000 --> 41:24.000
They were afraid, you know, if they made any false move

41:25.000 --> 41:28.000
they were sitting ducks there in the bay and…

41:28.000 --> 41:31.000
they patched it up

41:31.000 --> 41:33.000
and the St Kildans they all took to the hills, you know,

41:33.000 --> 41:35.000
when they saw it in the morning.

41:35.000 --> 41:37.000
So they patched it up and just 

41:37.000 --> 41:40.000
slid off gently at the next tide.

41:41.000 --> 41:46.000
And that was his daughter that was in Inverness

41:46.000 --> 41:49.000
and she said he was always on about St Kilda, you know,

41:49.000 --> 41:51.000
and she wanted to go there to… 

41:51.000 --> 41:54.000
I don’t know whether she got there or not

41:54.000 --> 41:58.000
but I put her in touch with Bruce Watt from Mallaig,

41:58.000 --> 42:01.000
he was running the National Trust cruises there. 

42:01.000 --> 42:07.000
So probably she landed up in St Kilda to see where her father was [laughs]

42:08.000 --> 42:14.000
but the gun was manned by a naval crew,

42:15.000 --> 42:18.000
but they decided against doing anything 

42:18.000 --> 42:22.000
about the submarine, because if they didn’t

42:22.000 --> 42:28.000
knock the submarine crew out with the first shot they would they would come out

42:28.000 --> 42:32.000
and kill the whole lot of them so…

42:33.000 --> 42:36.000
they were both thinking the same at the same time

42:36.000 --> 42:41.000
and they never told a soul until after the War.

42:42.000 --> 42:44.000
They just kept it quiet so that they,

42:44.000 --> 42:46.000
they would probably only be court-marshalled if they,

42:46.000 --> 42:51.000
if they knew they saw a submarine there on the beach and did nothing about it.

42:52.000 --> 42:54.000
Was it manned by local men?

42:54.000 --> 43:00.000
No, it was a Naval crew. Oh, could be from anywhere.

43:00.000 --> 43:02.000
There was only five of them there anyway so 

43:02.000 --> 43:04.000
they didn’t have much chance.

43:04.000 --> 43:11.000
But it’s in a school magazine: Eilean an Fhraoich Annual.

43:15.000 --> 43:20.000
It was probably done through the Nicolson Institute.

43:21.000 --> 43:26.000
There was this fellow from Tolsta in Lewis and… 

43:26.000 --> 43:30.000
[...]

43:30.000 --> 43:38.000
he was in the Merchant Service and when,

43:38.000 --> 43:40.000
it was during the First World War,

43:40.000 --> 43:46.000
he was in the Merchant Service and they got sunk

43:46.000 --> 43:49.000
by torpedoes and sunk by a submarine off,

43:49.000 --> 43:53.000
hundreds of miles off the west coast of Ireland.

43:54.000 --> 44:03.000
And they took to the lifeboats, and the German submarine

44:03.000 --> 44:04.000
surfaced and picked up the survivors.

44:07.000 --> 44:12.000
But they had picked up survivors from another ship

44:12.000 --> 44:16.000
before that and they were, they had no room, you know, for… 

44:16.000 --> 44:24.000
but within 24 hours they stopped this ship flying– 

44:24.000 --> 44:25.000
[BREAK IN TAPE]

44:25.000 --> 44:28.000
That was fair enough, they were getting out

44:28.000 --> 44:33.000
for exercise for an hour a day and that was…

44:33.000 --> 44:37.000
it was full of prisoners there, this Dutch vessel. 

44:38.000 --> 44:42.000
And they guessed it was heading for Germany,

44:43.000 --> 44:48.000
and it was a German ship in disguise.

44:51.000 --> 44:55.000
So this day when they were out on the deck for exercise, 

44:56.000 --> 45:02.000
this destroyer appeared, a British Navy destroyer

45:02.000 --> 45:08.000
appeared and stopped them, and checked their papers.

45:09.000 --> 45:12.000
Everything was in order.

45:13.000 --> 45:18.000
And they had rushed the prisoners below decks 

45:18.000 --> 45:27.000
before they… the navy boys boarded the ship.

45:31.000 --> 45:34.000
He climbed up the mast, he was the last,

45:34.000 --> 45:38.000
he hid somewhere and when they went below he climbed up the mast 

45:38.000 --> 45:46.000
and he started shouting, shouted in Gaelic that they were German,

45:46.000 --> 45:54.000
and there was Gaelic-speaking crew on the destroyer and they,

45:57.000 --> 46:00.000
they told the Captain, you know,

46:00.000 --> 46:03.000
what was happening, so they turned back and

46:03.000 --> 46:06.000
made a proper search and it was full of prisoners. 

46:06.000 --> 46:13.000
So they took them, they took them into, into Belfast,

46:14.000 --> 46:19.000
they took them into Belfast and they, they got 

46:19.000 --> 46:22.000
home from there and back to sea again.

46:23.000 --> 46:27.000
But years after the war, 

46:28.000 --> 46:33.000
there was a Klondyker in Stornoway, a German Klondyker in Stornoway,

46:33.000 --> 46:40.000
and this particular fellow was a crew member 

46:40.000 --> 46:47.000
on a fishing boat, and they were selling herring to this German Klondyker.

46:49.000 --> 46:53.000
And the Captain came out and he had one look at him and he says:

46:53.000 --> 46:57.000
“Do you know me?” and he says, “no, never seen you before.” 

46:57.000 --> 47:00.000
Well he says, “well I saw you, were you in the Merchant Service?”

47:00.000 --> 47:01.000
“Yes.”

47:01.000 --> 47:03.000
“Were you sunk off the west coast of Ireland?”

47:03.000 --> 47:04.000
“Yes.”

47:04.000 --> 47:08.000
“Well I was the Captain of that ship back then.” [laughs]

47:09.000 --> 47:11.000
It’s a small world…